          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The term
            %em topological conformal field theory
            (TCFT) is used for a linearization or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilization"} stabilization
            of something that is like a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            (CFT) up to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            and a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
          %blockquote
            %p
              (Actually, the remnant of conformal structure here should be just an artefact of the way to parameterize the moduli space of surfaces. As the classification result by Lurie discussed below shows, TCFTs are really
              = succeed "-TFTs.)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,2)
          %p
            This formalizes the physics notion of “the
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+string"} topological string
            %em topologically twisted
            superconformal field theory, such as, notably, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+mirror+symmetry"} homological mirror symmetry
          %p
            Recall that an ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            (CFT) is, in
            = succeed "-language," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+functor"} symmetric monoidal functor
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 2
                    %mi conf
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord_2^{conf}
            whose objects are disjoint unions of intervals and circles, and whose
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surface"} Riemann surface
          %p
            Since Riemann surfaces form a well-understood
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mi conf
                      %mo ,
                      %mi top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord_{2}^{conf,top}
            whose
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-space"} hom-space
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surface"} Riemann surface
          %p
            A “truly topological conformal field theory” would be an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mi conf
                      %mo ,
                      %mi top
                  %mo →
                  %mn ∞
                  %mi Grpd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Bord_2^{conf,top} \to \infty Grpd
          %p
            or similar. But what is actually called a “topological conformal field theory” is the linearization or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilization"} stabilization
            of this:
          %p
            in a TCFT, this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            of conformal
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
            whose
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-object"} hom-object
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-category"} dg-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"} homology
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-space"} hom-space
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mi conf
                      %mo ,
                      %mi dg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord_2^{conf,dg}
            for the resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-category"} dg-category
            of Riemann cobordisms. Then a TCFT is a an homotopy-symmetric monoidal
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+functor"} enriched functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mi conf
                      %mo ,
                      %mi dg
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F : Bord_2^{conf,dg} \to Ch_\bullet
          %p
            to the symmetric monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-category"} dg-category
            of chain complexes.
          %p
            This means in particular that when two
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surface"} Riemann surface
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_2
            are homologous as chains in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            of Riemann surfaces, then the TCFT will send them to two equivalent morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{\Sigma_1}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{\Sigma_2}
            of chain complexes between the in- and the output states. The equivalence between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{\Sigma_1}
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Σ
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{\Sigma_2}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_2
            as 0-chains in the moduli space. In particular, two non-homologous 1-chains between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_2
            will in general lead to non-equivalent equivalences between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{\Sigma_1}
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Σ
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{\Sigma_2}
          %h2#Definition Definition
          %p
            According to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+the+Classification+of+Topological+Field+Theories"} ClassTFT
            the original definition of the domain for TCFTs can be formulated as follows (without reference to any conformal or Riemann structure).
          %p
            %strong Definition
            The
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 2
                    %mi nc
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord^{nc}_2
            of non-compact 2-dimensional cobordism is defined as follows:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The objects of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Bord
                        %mn 2
                        %mi nc
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord^{nc}_2
                are oriented 0-manifolds.
            %li
              %p
                Given a pair of objects
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ∈
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Bord
                        %mn 2
                        %mi nc
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X, Y \in Bord^{nc}_2
                , a 1-morphism from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                is an oriented bordism
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                        %mo :
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B : X \to Y
            %li
              %p
                Given a pair of 1-morphsims
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo ′
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B,B' : X \to Y
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Bord
                          %mn 2
                          %mi nc
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord^{nc}_2
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B'
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Bord
                        %mn 2
                        %mi nc
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord^{nc}_2
                is an oriented bordism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma: B \to B'
                (which is trivial along
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                and
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                has nonempty intersection with
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                        %mo ′
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B'
            %li
              %p
                Higher morphisms in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Bord
                        %mn 2
                        %mi nc
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord^{nc}_2
                are given by (orientation preserving) diffeomorphisms, isotopies between diffeomorphisms, and so forth.
          %p
            Then, the
            = succeed "-theorem" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 2
                    %mi nc
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord^{nc}_2
            becomes
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C2%29-category"} symmetric monoidal (∞,2)-category
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C2%29-functor"} (∞,2)-functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo :
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Bord
                      %mn 2
                      %mi nc
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Z : Bord^{nc}_2 \to C
            %p
              are equivalent to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+objects"} Calabi-Yau objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              in
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
              sends the point to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            This is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+the+Classification+of+Topological+Field+Theories"} ClassTFT, theorem 4.2.11
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 2
                    %mi nc
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord^{nc}_2
            and the theorem above, obtaining a statement in terms of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} symmetric monoidal (∞,1)-categories
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Costello04"} Costello 04
          %p
            in the particular case
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi Ch
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C=Ch_\bullet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+the+Classification+of+Topological+Field+Theories"} ClassTFT
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{OC}
              be the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (infinity,1)-category
              of open-closed strings, described as follows:
            %ol
              %li
                %p objects are oriented 1-manifolds with boundary;
              %li
                %p morphisms are oriented bordisms between 1-manifolds such that each connected component has non-vanishing intersection with the codomain 1-manifold;
              %li
                %p
                  the higher morphisms are given by orientation preserving
                  = succeed "s," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
            %p
              Write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
              for the full
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sub-%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} sub-(∞,1)-category
              on disjoint unions of intervals (open strings sector).
          %p
            This is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+the+Classification+of+Topological+Field+Theories"} ClassTFT, above theorem 4.2.13
          %h2#Classification Classification
          %h3#with_coefficients_in_algebras_in_chain_complexes With coefficients in (algebras in) chain complexes
          %p
            The original statement of the classification result for TCFTs concerned symmetric homotopy-monoidal functors
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Bord
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %mi conf
                        %mo ,
                        %mi dg
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Ch
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord_2^{conf,dg} \to Ch_\bullet
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kevin+Costello"}> Costello
              , following
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Kontsevich
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  The category of open TCFTs with set
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Λ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
                  of D-branes is equivalent to that of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+categories"} Calabi-Yau categories
                  with set
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Λ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
                  of objects.
              %li
                %p
                  The homology of the chain complex of closed states of the universal extension of an open TCFT to an open-closed TCFT is the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+homology"} Hochschild homology
                  of the corresponding
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+category"} Calabi-Yau category
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Costello04"}> Costello 04
            ) this is proven using information about cell decompositions of the moduli space of punctured Riemann surfaces, thus effectively presenting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mi conf
                      %mo ,
                      %mi dg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord_2^{conf,dg}
            by generators-and-relations, The then theorem amounts to noticing that representations of these generators and relations define the operations in an
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
          %h3#general_version General version
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} symmetric monoidal (∞,1)-category
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Z : \mathcal{O} \to C
            %p
              are equivalent to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+algebra"} Calabi-Yau algebra
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+object"} objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              in
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
              sends the interval
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            This is the result of spring
            %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0412149"} Cos04
            reformulated and generalized according to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+the+Classification+of+Topological+Field+Theories"} ClassTFT, theorem 4.2.14
          %p
            This is a special case of the general
            = succeed "-theorem." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
          %p
            The idea of the proof is that a topological open string theory, i.e., a symmetric monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z : \mathcal{O} \to C
            has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extension"} Kan extension
            to an open-closed topological string theory, i.e., to a symmetric monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒪𝒞
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z : \mathcal{OC} \to C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C2%29-functor"} (∞,2)-functor
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 2
                    %mi nc
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord^{nc}_2
            to a symmetric monoidal
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,2)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C'
          %h2#ActionFunctionals Worldsheet and effective background theories
          %p
            One imagines generally that one obtains TCFTs, in their formal definition given above, from worldsheet
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
            and the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            and finding from it a collection of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            of bosonic field configurations.
          %p It seems there is at this point no literature giving a direct construction along these lines, but there is the following:
          %p
            In
            %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0605647"} Cos06
            is constructed from the geometric input datum of a generalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+space"} Calabi-Yau space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,Q)
            and it is shown that
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                there is a collection of differential forms
                = succeed "on" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi K
                          %mrow
                            %mi g
                            %mo ,
                            %mi h
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi ⋯
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_{g,h}(\cdots)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi ℳ
                        %mi g
                        %mrow
                          %mi h
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}_{g}^{h,n}
                of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surface"} Riemann surface
              %p (In the discussion leading up to Lemma 4.5.1 there. The proof that this yields a TCFT is theorem 4.5.4.)
            %li
              %p the partition function of the string perturbation series for the above TCFT is
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                          %mrow
                            %mrow
                              %mi g
                              %mo ,
                              %mi n
                              %mo ≥
                              %mn 0
                              %mo ,
                              %mi h
                              %mo &gt;
                              %mn 0
                          %mrow
                            %mrow
                              %mn 2
                              %mi g
                              %mo −
                              %mn 2
                              %mo +
                              %mi h
                              %mo +
                              %mfrac
                                %mi n
                                %mn 2
                      %msup
                        %mi λ
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi g
                          %mo −
                          %mn 2
                          %mo +
                          %mi h
                      %msup
                        %mi N
                        %mi h
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo !
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mrow
                          %msubsup
                            %mi ℳ
                            %mi g
                            %mrow
                              %mi h
                              %mo ,
                              %mi n
                      %msub
                        %mi K
                        %mrow
                          %mi g
                          %mo ,
                          %mi h
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi a
                        %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\sum_{{g,n \geq 0, h \gt 0} \atop {2g-2+h+\frac{n}{2}}}
                      \\lambda^{2g-2+h}N^h
                      \\frac{1}{n!}
                      \\int_{\mathcal{M}_g^{h,n}}
                      K_{g,h}(a^\otimes n)
              %p
                which is shown to be the partition function of a background
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
                coming from the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mi X
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mn 2
                      %mi a
                      %mi Q
                      %mi a
                      %mo +
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mn 3
                      %msup
                        %mi a
                        %mn 3
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      a \mapsto S(a) = \int_X \frac{1}{2} a Q a + \frac{1}{3}a^3
                      \\,.
          %p
            So this constructs a 2d TCFT and shows that its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective
            background quantum field theory is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            should give the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            considered above, is not explicitly considered here, this does formalizes at least some aspects of an observation that was earlier made in (
            %a{:href => "#Witten92"}> Witten 92
            ) where it was observed that Chern-Simons theory is the effective background string theory of 2d TFTs obtained from action functionals of the A-model and the B-model.
          %p
            Similarly the effective background QFT of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
            topological string can be identified. This is known as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kodeira-Spencer+gravity"} Kodeira-Spencer gravity
            or as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BCOV+theory"} BCOV theory
          %p
            (See also at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/world+sheets+for+world+sheets"} world sheets for world sheets
            for a similar mechanism and see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+1-brane+in+3d"} super 1-brane in 3d
            for related “physical” strings.)
          %p
            So via the detour over the effective background field theory, this sort of shows that the physicist’s A-model and B-model are indeed captured by the abstract
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            definition of TCFT as given above.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"}> A-model
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Landau-Ginzburg+model"} Landau-Ginzburg model
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+mirror+symmetry"} homological mirror symmetry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HCFT"} HCFT
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The concept is essentially a formalization of what used to be called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomological+field+theory"} cohomological field theory
            in
          %ul
            %li#Witten91
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Introduction to cohomological field theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/WittenCQFT.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p The definition was given independently by
          %ul
            %li#Getzler92
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ezra+Getzler"}> Ezra Getzler
              ,
              %em Batalin-Vilkovisky algebras and two-dimensional topological field theories
              , Comm. Math. Phys. 159(2), 265–285 (1994) (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9212043"}> arXiv:hep-th/9212043
              )
          %p and
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"}> Graeme Segal
              ,
              %em Topological field theory
              , (1999), Notes of lectures at Stanford university. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.cgtp.duke.edu/ITP99/segal/"}> web
              ). See in particular
              %a{:href => "http://www.cgtp.duke.edu/ITP99/segal/stanford/lect5.pdf"} lecture 5
              (“topological field theory with cochain values”).
          %p
            The classification of TCFTs by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+categories"} Calabi-Yau categories
            was discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Costello04
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kevin+Costello"}> Kevin Costello
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Topological conformal field theories and Calabi-Yau categories
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0412149"} arXiv:math/0412149
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kevin+Costello"}> Kevin Costello
                ,
                %em The Gromov-Witten potential associated to a TCFT
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0509264"}> arXiv:math/0509264
                )
          %p
            following conjectures by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"}> Maxim Kontsevich
              ,
              %em Homological algebra of mirror symmetry
              , in Proceedings of the International Congress of Mathematicians, Vol. 1, 2 (Zürich, 1994), pages 120–139, Basel, 1995, Birkhäuser.
          %p
            This classification is a precursor of the full
            = succeed "-theorem." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+the+Classification+of+Topological+Field+Theories"} On the Classification of Topological Field Theories
          %p Here are notes from a seminar on these definitions and results:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Teichner"} Peter Teichner
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kevin+Costello"} Kevin Costello
              %em TCFT seminar
              (
              %a{:href => "http://math.berkeley.edu/~cpries/Hot-Topics-07.pdf"}> pdf notes
              )
          %p
            Discussion of the construction of TCFTs from differential forms on moduli space and the way this induces by “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+quantization"}> second quantization
            ” effective background Chern-Simons theories is in
          %ul
            %li#Costello06
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kevin+Costello"}> Kevin Costello
              ,
              %em Topological conformal field theories and gauge theories
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0605647"}> arXiv:math/0605647
              )
          %p formalizing at least aspects of the observations in
          %ul
            %li#Witten92
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                ,
                %em Chern-Simons Gauge Theory As A String Theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9207094"}> arXiv:hep-th/9207094
                )
            %li
              %p
                P.A. Grassi,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giuseppe+Policastro"} Giuseppe Policastro
                %em Super-Chern-Simons Theory as Superstring Theory
                (
